1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,898
Working with several coding agents at once is powerful…

2
00:00:03,948 --> 00:00:05,252
until you lose track.

3
00:00:05,302 --> 00:00:06,736
Which agent is on what?

4
00:00:06,786 --> 00:00:10,800
Is it still working, is it stuck, or did it finish an hour ago?

5
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:14,670
And when it says it's done, how do you know it really works?

6
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:18,746
Cello is a task board built for exactly that.

7
00:00:18,796 --> 00:00:20,412
It runs on your own machine,

8
00:00:20,462 --> 00:00:23,683
and your agents use it like teammates: they take cards,

9
00:00:23,733 --> 00:00:27,430
show you their work as it happens, and hand it back with proof.

10
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:31,600
To get started, point your coding agent at Cello,

11
00:00:31,650 --> 00:00:33,918
and ask it to set it up for your project.

12
00:00:33,968 --> 00:00:36,461
It installs Cello, starts it on your machine,

13
00:00:36,511 --> 00:00:38,779
and connects your project to a new board.

14
00:00:38,829 --> 00:00:39,514
From then on,

15
00:00:39,564 --> 00:00:42,510
every agent session in that project works through it.

16
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:50,700
Setup also gives your agent two new commands.

17
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:53,862
For real work, start a session with a strong model,

18
00:00:53,912 --> 00:00:56,715
like Claude Opus on high or extra-high effort,

19
00:00:56,765 --> 00:01:00,497
and run the orchestrate command with the tasks you want done.

20
00:01:00,547 --> 00:01:02,853
That session becomes the orchestrator.

21
00:01:02,903 --> 00:01:03,411
It plans,

22
00:01:03,461 --> 00:01:08,061
and hands each task to a builder agent working in its own copy of the code.

23
00:01:08,111 --> 00:01:11,906
A fresh reviewer agent checks every change before it's merged.

24
00:01:11,956 --> 00:01:14,820
The orchestrator doesn't write the code itself…

25
00:01:14,870 --> 00:01:15,750
it coordinates.

26
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,900
When you ask for something, it becomes a card.

27
00:01:19,950 --> 00:01:23,666
An agent claims it, and you watch it move: who's working on it,

28
00:01:23,716 --> 00:01:27,313
whether it's active right now, and what stage the work is in,

29
00:01:27,363 --> 00:01:29,465
from building, to review, to fixing.

30
00:01:29,515 --> 00:01:31,916
Every change records which agent made it,

31
00:01:31,966 --> 00:01:33,470
and in which conversation.

32
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:37,311
When an agent needs a decision from you,

33
00:01:37,361 --> 00:01:40,729
it doesn't wait in a terminal you might not be watching.

34
00:01:40,779 --> 00:01:44,268
It asks on the card, with the options, its recommendation,

35
00:01:44,318 --> 00:01:44,757
and why.

36
00:01:44,807 --> 00:01:47,686
And you can write to it anytime, right in Cello:

37
00:01:47,736 --> 00:01:50,005
a comment on the card, or on a review,

38
00:01:50,055 --> 00:01:52,751
reaches the orchestrator's session by itself…

39
00:01:52,801 --> 00:01:56,230
and it passes your words on to the agents doing the work.

40
00:01:57,680 --> 00:01:59,156
And when the work is done,

41
00:01:59,206 --> 00:02:01,798
the agent can't just move the card to review.

42
00:02:01,848 --> 00:02:05,321
It hands you a review packet: what changed, how to check it,

43
00:02:05,371 --> 00:02:08,080
and the evidence, like a video of the real app.

44
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:10,663
You approve it, or send it back with a note…

45
00:02:10,713 --> 00:02:13,070
and the note reaches the agent's session.

46
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:17,272
Everything stays local: one small server,

47
00:02:17,322 --> 00:02:19,869
and one database file on your machine.

48
00:02:19,919 --> 00:02:21,373
No accounts, no cloud,

49
00:02:21,423 --> 00:02:24,790
and every change shows up on your board instantly.

50
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:26,878
Cello.

51
00:02:26,928 --> 00:02:29,630
Your agents, in harmony.

